<h2>What is the ISFDB?</h2>
The ISFDB is a continuous, ongoing effort to catalog works of Speculative 
Fiction, and to make that information freely available to the public.
It consists of a database compiler, a database engine, and the actual data 
files themselves.  The database contains (as of this writing) about 100000 
titles (with about 21000 novels, 50000 short stories, with the rest 
miscellaneous), 20000 authors, and roughly 18000 award entries.
<p>
The ISFDB engine is a collection of run-time binaries which produce htmls
upon request. While most web sites have web pages spinning on a disk somewhere,
the ISFDB constructs web pages on demand from a database. It has the ability 
to search the database via author, title, year, or series name. It can also 
be used to generate a yearly fiction index, view the contents of a specific 
magazine, or generate an awards page for a given award and given year.  Searches 
lead to a list of author anchors, each of which are hot-links to a specific 
author's bibliography. The various page types available from the ISFDB engine 
are:
<ol>
  <li><i><a name="1A">The Summary Bibliography</a></i>.
  This lists the author's legal name, 
  birthplace, date-of-birth, date-of-death, an awards summary, and a 
  listing of the author's works by the following categories: series,
  novels, collections, anthology series, stand-alone anthologies, works
  of non-fiction, and short fiction. An example layout of the author
  information is:
  <pre>
    <i>Working Name</i> - Bibliography Summary
    -------------------------------------------------------
    [<b>Pub Biblio</b>][<b>Alpha</b>][<b>Main Menu</b>][<b>Search</b>][<b>Correct Author Info</b>] 
    -------------------------------------------------------
    <i>Legal Name</i>  (<i>Birthplace</i>;<i>Residence</i>, <i>Birthdate</i>-<i>Deathdate</i>)

    {<i>Pseudonym listings</i>}
    {<i>Awards Summary</i>}
    {<i>Bibliographic Data</i>}
  </pre>
  The <i>Pub Biblio</i> hotlink leads to the publication bibliography, while
  the <i>Alpha</i> button creates a summary bibliography with the entries sorted 
  in alphabetical order. The <i>Main Menu</i> button brings up the top-level 
  page of the database. <i>Search</i> takes you to the main search page. The
  <i>Correct Author Info</i> allows you to update the author's recorded legal name,
  birthplace, birthyear, deathyear, pseudonyms, email addresses, and web page
  addresses.
  <p>
  An actual example:
  <pre>
    Cordwainer Smith - Bibliography Summary
    -------------------------------------------------------
    [<b>Pub Biblio</b>][<b>Alpha</b>][<b>Main Menu</b>][<b>Search</b>][<b>Correct Author Info</b>] 
    -------------------------------------------------------
    Linebarger, Paul Myron Anthony  (USA, 1913-1966)

    (pseudonyms: Karloman Jungahr; Carmichael Smith) 
  </pre>
  The author data is followed by the following bibliographic sections 
  (in this order): Series, Novels, Collections, Anthology Series, 
  Anthologies, Non-Fiction, Short Fiction, Poems, Non-Genre, Artwork, 
  and Serials.
  <p>
  Each work has a date of first 
  publication citation. Works for which dates are not currently known 
  will list the date as (<i>unknown</i>), dates for books which will 
  soon be published list the date as (<i>forthcoming</i>), while dates
  for famous works which, for various reasons, have never been seen in
  public list the date as (<i>unpublished</i>).
  If the work has a collaborator, a collaboration citation is listed as 
  <i>with</i> <b>author</b>, where <b>author</b> is a hot-link to the 
  collaborator's Summary Bibliography.
  If the work received an award nomination or win, an award citation of
  the form <b>[AwYear]</b> is listed, where Aw is the award abbreviation. 
  Keys to award abbreviations are listed with the award summary at the top
  of the author's Summary Bibliography. Award nominees that did not win 
  are denoted by the citation <b>[AwYear n]</b>.  If the database has
  publication data for a particular title, the title will then be a
  hot-link to the publication data. A partial example citation (with
  hot-links in bold) from Ellen Datlow's bibliography:
  <pre>
 Anthologies
     <b>Alien Sex</b> (1990)
     <b>Black Thorn, White Rose</b> (1994) with <b>Terri Windling</b> [<b>Wf1995 n</b>]
     <b>Little Deaths</b> (1994)[<b>Wf1995</b>]
     Snow White, Blood Red (1994) with <b>Terri Windling</b> [<b>Wf1994 n</b>]
  </pre>
  If the work was written under a pseudonym, a pseudonym citation is listed
  <i>[as author]</i>. An example from Joe Haldeman's bibliography:
  <pre>
 Series
    Attar
        War of Nerves (1975) [as Robert Graham]
        Attar's Revenge (1975) [as Robert Graham]
  </pre>
  Pseudonym citations are not hot-links. Award citations are hot-links to:
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="1B">The Yearly Awards Page</a></i>.
  This page lists all of the nominees
  for a specific award for a specific year. The nominees are listed by category
  (<i>Novels</i>, <i>Novellas</i>, etc...), with the winner annotated with
  an <b>o</b>. The Locus Poll Award differs in that nominees are numbered and
  presented in their poll order, with the poll winner marked with a bold <b>1</b>.
  Nominees and winners are listed as title/author pairs, where the author is
  a hot-link to a Summary Bibliography. Awards currently supported are: 
  <i>Aurora</i>,
  <i>Bram Stoker</i>,
  <i>Campbell</i>,
  <i>Clarke</i>,
  <i>Hugo</i>,
  <i>James Tiptree, Jr.</i>,
  <i>Locus Poll</i>,
  <i>Nebula</i>,
  <i>P. K. Dick</i>, and
  <i>WFA</i>.
  There are two additional anchors at the bottom of each yearly award page:
  <b>[Previous Year]</b> and <b>[Next Year]</b>, which do exactly what you think
  they might do. Partial example from the 1995 Hugo Award page:
<pre>
Novel
o  Mirror Dance                    <b>Lois McMaster Bujold</b>
   Towing Jehovah                  <b>James Morrow</b>
   Mother of Storms                <b>John Barnes</b>
   Brittle Innings                 <b>Michael Bishop</b>
   Beggars and Choosers            <b>Nancy Kress</b>
</pre>
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="1C">The Publication History</a></i>.
  If a title on the Bibliography Summary has publication data entered into the
  database, the title will appear as a hotlink to its publication history.
  If this link is selected, all publication data for that title will be 
  displayed, but for that title only. 
  The form of each publication citation
  is [title]/listed author/[publication summary]. For instance, the publication
  citations for Brin's <i>Startide Rising</i> are:

<pre>
For: Startide Rising

    1. <b>Startide Rising</b>, David Brin, 1985, Phantasia Press, 0932096387, $18.00, hc
    2. <b>Startide Rising</b>, David Brin, 1985, Phantasia Press, 0-932096-38-7, $45.00
    3. <b>Earthclan</b>, David Brin, 1987, Doubleday, 1-56865-037-X, hc
    4. <b>Startide Rising</b>, David Brin, 1991, Bantam UK, 0-553-17170-4, £4.99, pb
    5. <b>Startide Rising</b>, David Brin, 1993, Bantam Spectra, 0-553-27418-X, $5.99, pb
    6. <b>Startide Rising</b>, David Brin, 1996, Orbit, 1-85723-372-7, £5.99, pb 
</pre>

  The titles in the publication citations are hot-links to <i>A
  Content Listing</i> for the specific publication selected.
  A complete publication bibliography can be generated by selecting:
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="1D">The Publication Bibliography</a></i>.
  For each author work, all 
  publication information is displayed. This page is reached from the 
  <b>[Pub Biblio]</b> anchor on the author's Summary Bibliography.
  The entries on the page are in a format identical to the Publication
  History above.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="1E">A Content Listing</a></i>.
  This page displays all publication
  data (such as cover artist and notes), as well as a content listing of 
  the publication. For an omnibus, the content listing will be the novels 
  contained within the omnibus; for a short
  story collection, anthology, or zine, the content listing will be the story
  titles found within the publication. The author anchor of each title/author
  pair found in the content listing is a hot-link to the author's Summary 
  Bibliography. An example content listing of the Jan 86 issue of Asimov's
  Science Fiction:

<pre>
For: <i>Isaac Asimov's Science Fiction Magazine, Jan 1986</i>

  Auth/Ed: Gardner Dozois
  Year: 1986
  Price: $2.00
  Cover: <b>Hisaki Yasuda</b>  for "Count Zero"
  Notes: Vol. 10 No 1 (Whole Number 100)

Contains the following works:

<i>Serials</i>
Count Zero (Part I)                               <b>William Gibson</b> 

<i>Short Stories</i>
The Eye of the Beholder                           <b>Isaac Asimov</b> 
Jeff Beck                                         <b>Lewis Shiner</b> 
Pretty Boy Crossover                              <b>Pat Cadigan</b> 
Reduction                                         <b>John Kessel</b> and
                                                  <b>Gregory Frost</b> 
Special Education                                 <b>Tim Sullivan</b> 

<i>Poems</i>
Harmony of the Sphere at Spion Kop                <b>Roger Meador</b> 
Interstellar Dust                                 <b>Peter Payack</b> 
Moonburn                                          <b>Peter Payack</b> 
A Quotella For Ted Sturgeon                       <b>Robert Frazier</b> 
A Trivial Matter                                  <b>Peter Payack</b> 
Why There Is Now (And Most Probably Always Will
  Be) A Shortage of Subatomic Physicists          <b>Peter Payack</b> 
</pre>

  <p>
  <li><i><a name="1H">Forthcoming Titles</a></i>.
  This page lists all known forthcoming
  titles, sorted by month. An entry in the left-hand margin indicates whether
  the title is a Novel, Collection, Anthology, or work of Non-fiction. Again,
  author anchors are hot-links to the author's Summary Bibliography.

  <p>
  <li><i><a name="1I">Fiction Index</a></i>.
   This feature creates a fiction index in a format similar to Dozois' or
   Datlow's Honorable Mentions in their yearly Best-Of anthologies. Titles
   are collated by author; authors are sorted by last name, novels are in
   italics; shortfiction is double-quoted with the location of its 
   publication (if known). The authors are hot-links to bibliographies;
   the publication date is a hot-link to a content listing for that
   publication. Publications which are collections or anthologies are
   enclosed in brackets. A partial example from the 1995 fiction index:

<pre>
<b>George Alec Effinger</b> 
    "Maureen Birmbaum at the Looming Awfulness", F&SF, <b>March</b> 
    "One", [<b>New Legends</b>] (Tor, hc)
<b>Greg Egan</b> 
    Distress, (Millennium, hc)
    "A Kidnapping", [<b>Axiomatic</b>] (Orion Books, pb)
    "Luminous", Asimov's, <b>September</b> 
    "Mister Volition", Interzone, <b>October</b> 
    "Mitochondrial Eve", Interzone, <b>February</b> 
    "Seeing", [<b>Axiomatic</b>] (Orion Books, pb)
    "Silver Fire", Interzone, <b>December</b> 
    "TAP", Asimov's, <b>November</b> 
    "Wang's Carpets", [<b>New Legends</b>] (Tor, hc)
<b>Suzette Haden Elgin</b> 
    "Only a Housewife", F&SF, <b>September</b> 
<b>Kandis Elliot</b> 
    "The Androgyne Murders", SF Age, <b>November</b> 
    "Mother Nature Visits the Zoo", Tomorrow, <b>October</b> 
    "Road Kills", Asimov's, <b>February</b> 
    "Sith Season", Asimov's, <b>May</b> 
</pre>
</ol>

Additionally there are a number of useful top-level htmls;

<ol>
  <li> A Forms Page to query the ISFDB for an awards page for a
       specific year.
  <li> A Magazine page, which leads to a month-by-month listing of
       Magazine contents. Currently tracked magazines are: Absolute
       Magnitude, Analog Science Fiction and Fact, Asimov's Science 
       Fiction, Century, Crank!, Fantasy & Science Fiction, 
       Interzone Science Fiction and Fantasy, Marion Zimmer Bradley's Fantasy,
       Omni Online, Pirate Writings, Realms of Fantasy, Science Fiction 
       Age, and Tomorrow Speculative Fiction.
  <li> The latest features of the ISFDB engine.
  <li> The FAQ for the ISFDB.
  <li> The Bibliographic Sources used for the ISFDB.
  <li> Some statistics pages.
</ol>

